data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": "The statement is the famous **Twin Prime Conjecture**:\n\n\\[\n\\text{There are infinitely many primes } p \\text{ such that } p+2 \\text{ is also prime.}\n\\]\n\nThe honest mathematical answer is: **this remains an open problem**. It has neither been proved nor disproved. So no one can currently give a valid proof or disproof.\n\nBelow is a"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " step-by-step explanation of why this is difficult and what is known.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the statement says\n\nTwin primes are pairs of primes that differ by 2:\n\n\\[\n(3,5),\\ (5,7),\\ (11,13),\\ (17,19),\\ (29,31),\\ (41,43),\\dots\n\\]\n\nThe conjecture says that this list never ends.\n\nThere are currently many known twin primes, including enormously large ones, but finding examples is not the same as proving there are infinitely many.\n\n---\n\n## 2. A necessary condition: primes greater than 3\n\nEvery prime greater than 3 is congruent to either:\n\n"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": "\\[\n1 \\pmod 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 5 \\pmod 6\n\\]\n\nbecause any other residue class modulo 6 is divisible by 2 or 3.\n\nNow suppose \\(p > 3\\) and both \\(p\\) and \\(p+2\\) are prime.\n\n- If \\(p \\equiv 1 \\pmod 6\\), then\n\n\\[\np+2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod 6,\n\\]\n\nso \\(p+2\\) would be divisible by 3, and since \\(p+2 > 3\\), it cannot be prime.\n\n- Therefore we must have\n\n\\[\np \\equiv 5 \\pmod 6,\n\\]\n\nso every twin prime pair beyond \\((3,5)\\) has the form\n\n\\[\n(6n-1,\\ 6n+1).\n\\]\n\nThis narrows down the possible candidates, but it does not prove infinitely many exist.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why standard arguments do not prove it"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": "\n\n### Euclid’s proof of infinitely many primes\n\nEuclid proved that there are infinitely many primes by assuming a finite list of primes, multiplying them all together, adding 1, and producing a new prime not in the list.\n\nThat argument works for primes individually, but it does **not** produce a new pair of primes separated by 2. Multiplying all known twin primes and adding or subtracting constants does not force both \\(N\\) and \\(N+2\\) to be prime.\n\n### Dirichlet’s theorem\n\nDirichlet’s theorem says that if \\(\\gcd(a,q)=1\\), then there are infinitely many primes of the form\n\n\\[\na + qn.\n\\]\n\nFor example, there are infinitely many primes congruent to"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " \\(5 \\pmod 6\\), and infinitely many primes congruent to \\(1 \\pmod 6\\).\n\nBut Dirichlet’s theorem only guarantees that each arithmetic progression contains infinitely many primes separately. It does **not** guarantee that the same value of \\(n\\) makes both\n\n\\[\n6n-1\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n6n+1\n\\]\n\nprime at the same time.\n\n---\n\n## 4. What partial results are known?\n\nSeveral major theorems come close to the twin prime conjecture, but do not settle it.\n\n### Chen’s theorem\n\nChen Jingrun proved:\n\n\\[\n\\text{There are infinitely many primes } p \\text{ such that } p+2 \\text{ has at most two prime factors.}\n\\]\n\nThat is, infinitely often \\(p+2\\) is either prime"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " or a semiprime — the product of two primes.\n\nThis is impressive, but it is weaker than requiring \\(p+2\\) itself to be prime.\n\n### Bounded gaps between primes\n\nIn 2013, Yitang Zhang proved that there is some finite bound \\(H\\) such that infinitely many pairs of primes differ by at most \\(H\\). The original bound was about 70 million. Later work by James Maynard and the Polymath project improved this to:\n\n\\[\n246\n\\]\n\nwith further improvements possible under certain conjectures.\n\nThis proves"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " that primes are often close together, but it does **not** prove that the gap 2 occurs infinitely often.\n\n### Brun’s sieve\n\nThe Norwegian mathematician Viggo Brun proved that the sum of reciprocals of twin primes converges:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p,\\ p+2 \\text{ prime}}} \\left(\\frac1p + \\frac1{p+2}\\right) < \\infty.\n\\]\n\nThis is a famous result, but it does **not** imply that there are only finitely many twin primes. An infinite set can have a convergent sum of reciprocals.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Heuristic evidence\n\nA rough probabilistic model gives strong evidence that the twin prime conjecture should"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " be true.\n\nThe probability that a random integer near \\(n\\) is prime is approximately\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\log n}.\n\\]\n\nIf primality of \\(n\\) and \\(n+2\\) were approximately independent, the chance that both are prime would be about\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{(\\log n)^2}.\n\\]\n\nSumming this over all \\(n\\) gives a divergent series:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{(\\log n)^2} = \\infty.\n\\]\n\nThat suggests there should be infinitely many twin primes. In fact, the expected number of twin primes up to \\(x\\) is conjecturally:\n\n\\[\n\\pi_2(x) \\sim 2C_2 \\int"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": "_2^x \\frac{dt}{(\\log t)^2},\n\\]\n\nwhere\n\n\\[\nC_2 = \\prod_{q \\geq 3} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{(q-1)^2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nis the “twin prime constant.”\n\nHowever, this is only a heuristic argument, not a proof.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe statement\n\n\\[\n\\text{“There are infinitely many primes } p \\text{ such that } p+2 \\text{ is also prime.”}\n\\]\n\nis one of the oldest unsolved problems in number theory.\n\nSo the correct answer to “prove or disprove” is:\n\n> **Neither is"}

data: {"type": "text-delta", "id": "text-0", "delta": " currently possible. The twin prime conjecture remains open.**\n\nAs of my last reliable mathematical knowledge, no accepted proof or disproof has been found. It is widely believed to be true, but belief is not proof."}

data: {"type": "text-end", "id": "text-0"}

